module

public import WeilConjectures.FieldTheory.SeparableExtension
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.TensorProduct.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.AtPrime.Basic
import WeilConjectures.FieldTheory.SeparablyGeneratedTensorReduced
import WeilConjectures.FieldTheory.GeometricallyReducedExtensionSeparable
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.Basic
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Artinian.Module
import Mathlib.FieldTheory.SeparablyGenerated

/-! # Prime localizations of a separable field tensored with a finite extension

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tags 030W, 04KN, 00J6 and 00JA
(blueprint: lem-sfb-separable-residue-finite-tensor-fields).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Algebra

/-- A separable, possibly transcendental extension tensored with a finite extension is a
nonzero finite product of fields. Every prime localization is separable over the right
field, using the structural map `e ↦ (1 ⊗ e)/1`.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tags 030W, 04KN, 00J6 and 00JA
(blueprint: lem-sfb-separable-residue-finite-tensor-fields).

Proof:
1. For any field extension H/k, a nilpotent of F tensor H involves only finitely many
   elements of F. They generate a finitely generated intermediate field F₀/k, which is
   separably generated by def-weil-pbc-separable-field-extension. The theorem
   lem-weil-pbc-separably-generated-tensor-reduced makes F₀ tensor H reduced. The inclusion
   into F tensor H is injective by lem-weil-pbc-module-over-field-flat and
   lem-trf-flat-tensor-preserves-injection, so the original nilpotent is zero (Tag 030U).
2. A finite k-basis of E gives an F-spanning set of F tensor E. Thus
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-dim-algebra-artinian makes this tensor Artinian (Tag 00J6).
   It is reduced by step 1 and nonzero by lem-sfb-field-extension-tensor-injective.
3. Apply lem-sfb-reduced-artinian-residue-product to decompose the tensor into its finitely
   many maximal-ideal quotients. These are fields, and there is at least one factor since
   the tensor is nonzero. The quotient maps preserve the E-action (Tags 00J7 and 00JB).
4. In a finite product of fields every prime contains all but one coordinate idempotent.
   It is the kernel of that coordinate projection. Localizing gives that field: the map
   sends a/s to aᵢ/sᵢ and its inverse sends b to the coordinate vector b eᵢ/1. These maps
   respect the E-action from def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime (Tag 00JA).
5. For every H/E, tensor reassociation identifies (F tensor_k E) tensor_E H with
   F tensor_k H, reduced by step 1. Each field factor's coordinate inclusion is an
   E-linear multiplicative map with a linear retraction. After tensoring with H it is
   still injective, so the field factor tensor_E H is reduced. The inclusion need not
   preserve 1; multiplicativity suffices for this reducedness argument (Tag 04KN).
6. Apply lem-weil-pbc-char-zero-field-extension-separable in characteristic zero, and
   lem-weil-pbc-geometrically-reduced-extension-separable in prime characteristic
   (Tag 030W). Each field factor is separable over E. The E-algebra isomorphism in step 4
   transports finitely generated intermediate fields and separating transcendence bases,
   giving the asserted separability of every localization with its stipulated E-map.
-/
theorem separableExtension_finite_tensorProduct_fields
    (k : Type u) (F : Type v) (E : Type w)
    [Field k] [Field F] [Field E] [Algebra k F] [Algebra k E]
    [FiniteDimensional k E] (hF : IsSeparableExtension k F) :
    Nontrivial (F ⊗[k] E) ∧
      (∃ (n : ℕ), 0 < n ∧
        ∃ (L : Fin n → Type (max v w)) (hL : ∀ i, Field (L i)),
          letI := hL
          Nonempty ((F ⊗[k] E) ≃+* (∀ i, L i))) ∧
      ∀ (P : Ideal (F ⊗[k] E)) [P.IsPrime],
        ∃ hP : IsField (Localization.AtPrime P),
          letI := hP.toField
          letI := ((algebraMap (F ⊗[k] E) (Localization.AtPrime P)).comp
            (TensorProduct.includeRight (R := k) (A := F) (B := E)).toRingHom).toAlgebra
          IsSeparableExtension E (Localization.AtPrime P) := by
  sorry

end Algebra
